  {
   "name": "Why Your Engine Does Not Melt",
   "lines": [
    [
     "c09_1",
     {
      "label": "hook",
      "head": "Most of your fuel becomes *heat*",
      "pose": "point",
      "face": "wow",
      "prop": {
       "type": "split",
       "center": "FUEL",
       "parts": [
        [
         "MOVEMENT",
         30,
         "#2f9e5b"
        ],
        [
         "HEAT + LOSSES",
         70,
         "#f0502a"
        ]
       ],
       "hi": 1
      }
     },
     "A petrol engine turns only around a third of its fuel into movement. Most of the rest becomes heat."
    ],
    [
     "c09_2",
     {
      "label": "coolant",
      "head": "*Coolant* carries heat away",
      "pose": "present",
      "face": "smile",
      "prop": {
       "type": "cooling"
      }
     },
     "Coolant flows through passages around the cylinders, soaking up heat."
    ],
    [
     "c09_3",
     {
      "label": "pump",
      "head": "The *water pump* keeps it moving",
      "pose": "count",
      "face": "smile",
      "prop": {
       "type": "cooling"
      }
     },
     "A water pump, driven by the engine or an electric motor, keeps it circulating."
    ],
    [
     "c09_4",
     {
      "label": "radiator",
      "head": "The *radiator* dumps the heat",
      "pose": "present",
      "face": "grin",
      "prop": {
       "type": "cooling"
      }
     },
     "Hot coolant flows through the radiator, where air rushing through thin fins carries the heat away. A fan helps when you are stopped."
    ],
    [
     "c09_5",
     {
      "label": "thermostat",
      "head": "The *thermostat* is the gatekeeper",
      "pose": "think",
      "face": "think",
      "prop": {
       "type": "cooling",
       "open": false
      }
     },
     "When the engine is cold, the thermostat stays shut so it warms up fast. At around ninety degrees, it opens."
    ],
    [
     "c09_6",
     {
      "label": "danger",
      "head": "Overheating can *wreck* an engine",
      "pose": "shy",
      "face": "worried",
      "prop": {
       "type": "stat",
       "big": "PULL OVER",
       "sub": "IF THE TEMPERATURE GAUGE HITS RED",
       "col": "#f0502a"
      }
     },
     "Overheating can warp the cylinder head and blow the head gasket. If the temperature gauge climbs high, pull over safely."
    ],
    [
     "c09_7",
     {
      "label": "safety",
      "head": "Never open a *hot* radiator cap",
      "pose": "facepalm",
      "face": "worried",
      "prop": {
       "type": "checklist",
       "items": [
        [
         "Open the cap when hot",
         false
        ],
        [
         "Wait until it cools",
         true
        ],
        [
         "Check level when cold",
         true
        ]
       ]
      }
     },
     "The system is under pressure. Opening it hot can spray scalding coolant. Always wait for it to cool down."
    ],
    [
     "c09_8",
     {
      "label": "takeaway",
      "head": "Pump, radiator, *thermostat*",
      "pose": "thumbs",
      "face": "grin",
      "prop": {
       "type": "cooling"
      }
     },
     "Pump it round, cool it in the radiator, and let the thermostat decide when."
    ]
   ]
  }
